# From rule to scenario

### Given, When, Then, bound to real code

Week 7, rung 4 of 7 (Proven), talk 1

---

## Where we are on the ladder

| Rung | Variant | Weeks |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 Seed, 2 Lite, 3 Layers | | 1 to 5 |
| **4 Proven** | **`rung-4-proven`** | 6, **7 (this week, the last on rung 4)** |
| 5 Reader | `rung-5-reader` | 8, 9 |
| 6 Guarded, 7 Shipped, Summit | | 10 to 12 |

Last week: the sentence. This week: its proof, and the proof of the proof.

---

## BDD in one slide

**Behaviour-Driven Development** (Dan North, 2006): describe behaviour in the language of the people who
want it, as examples, before the code.

- a **feature** groups the behaviour of one part of the product
- a **Rule** states one requirement (Gherkin 6 added `Rule:`)
- a **scenario** is one example that proves it: **Given** a context, **When** something happens, **Then**
  an outcome you can observe

The examples are executable. A step definition binds each line to code.

---

## The anatomy of a feature

```gherkin
# variants/rung-4-proven/tests/bdd/features/site/static-site.feature
@developer
Feature: A Course becomes a static website
  As a student reading a course without a server
  I want a page for each thing I read, linked the way upstream's tutors-lite links them
  So that I can read the course from a folder of HTML

  @R-010 @ears-event-driven
  Rule: When a lab has more than one step, the site shall link each step page to the step before it
        and the step after it.

    Scenario: The middle step links both ways
      Given a course with a lab whose steps are "Intro, Setup, Build"
      When the site is built
      Then the step page "Setup.html" links to the previous page "index.html" and the next page "Build.html"
      And the step page "index.html" has no previous link
      And the step page "Build.html" has no next link
```

---

## A Rule cites its requirement

- `@R-010`: the id, exactly one, from `REQUIREMENTS.md`
- `@ears-event-driven`: the pattern, exactly the one the row names
- the `Rule:` title: the sentence, word for word

The closure audit checks all three. A scenario outside any Rule fails it too (`scenario-outside-rule`): it
would prove nothing anyone asked for.

Upstream is the same shape, with four-digit ids:

```gherkin
@rule-0265 @ears-event-driven
Rule: When a lecturer builds a lab, tutors shall write one step per markdown file in file-name order, ...
```

---

## Good scenarios

| Do | Because |
| --- | --- |
| one behaviour per scenario | a failure names one thing |
| Given says only what matters to this Rule | the rest is in a helper (`tests/bdd/support/course.ts`) |
| When is one action | the thing the requirement's trigger names |
| Then says what is observable | a page, a field, a status, a message |
| use the domain's words | "a lab whose steps are", not "a JSON array of length 3" |
| one example that would fail if the rule were broken | otherwise it proves nothing |

From EARS: a state or feature becomes a Given; an event a When; an unwanted condition a When that goes
wrong.

---

## Step definitions

```ts
// variants/rung-4-proven/tests/bdd/steps/site/static-site.steps.ts
const feature = await loadFeature("tests/bdd/features/site/static-site.feature");

describeFeature(feature, ({ Rule }) => {
  Rule("When a lab has more than one step, the site shall link each step page ...", ({ RuleScenario }) => {
    RuleScenario("The middle step links both ways", ({ Given, When, Then, And }) => {
      Given("a course with a lab whose steps are {string}", (_ctx, names: string) => {
        const steps: Lab["los"] = list(names).map((name) => ({ type: "step", id: name, ... }));
        course = courseOf({ ...lo("book-1"), type: "lab", los: steps });
      });
      When("the site is built", built);
      Then("the step page {string} links to the previous page {string} and the next page {string}", ...);
```

`{string}` captures quoted text. `list("Intro, Setup, Build")` splits it.

---

## vitest-cucumber is strict, both ways

| You do | It fails with |
| --- | --- |
| add a Rule to the feature, not to the steps | `RuleNotCalledError: Rule: ... was not called` |
| change a Rule's title in the feature only | `FeatureUknowRuleError: Rule: ... does not exist` |
| add a step line with no binding | an error naming the missing step |

Every Given, When and Then is its own vitest test, so `pnpm test:bdd` counts steps: 61 for 12 Rules. Steps
call **product code** (`buildCourse`, `buildSite`, `run`), never a fixture's own answer.

---

## Specify first: the loop from now on

1. **Take an id.** `pnpm test:ears:audit --next-id` prints `R-013`.
2. **Write the sentence** in `REQUIREMENTS.md`. A person says yes.
3. **Write the Rule and a scenario.** The audit is satisfied; vitest-cucumber is not.
4. **Bind the steps. See red.** It must fail for the right reason.
5. **Make it green** in the layer the row names.
6. **Pin the code** with a unit or property test, and hold the mutation floors.
7. **Reflect.** Kaizen.

This is standard work. Every lab from week 7 to week 11 opens with steps 1 to 4.

---

## Red, for the right reason

```
 FAIL  tests/bdd/steps/site/static-site.steps.ts > Feature: A Course becomes a static website >
   Rule: When a lab has more than one step, the site shall label each step page with its position,
   such as Step 2 of 3. > Scenario: Each step knows where it is >
   Then the step page "Setup.html" says "Step 2 of 3"
AssertionError: expected '<!doctype html>\n<html lang="en">\n<h…' to contain 'Step 2 of 3'
      Tests  1 failed | 63 passed (64)
```

Not a typo, not a crash in the Given: the Then fails because the behaviour is missing. A scenario you have
never seen fail has not shown that it can.

---

## Background, outlines, and lists

Upstream's `tests/bdd/features/generator/course-json.feature`:

```gherkin
  Background:
    Given the synthetic corpus course has been generated

  @rule-0267 @ears-event-driven
  Rule: When a course folder holds properties.yaml, calendar.yaml or enrollment.yaml, tutors shall copy ...
    Scenario Outline: Course YAML is copied as written
      Then "<key>" in the tutors.json equals the course's "<file>"
      Examples:
        | key        | file            |
        | properties | properties.yaml |
```

A `Background` is a Given every scenario shares. An outline runs one scenario per row. Rung 4 passes lists
as one quoted string instead, which keeps each scenario readable on one line.

---

## The scenario proves; it does not pin

The scenario for R-013 passes. Then Stryker mutates the new line, `lab.los.length > 1` to `false`, and the
mutant **survives**. Why?

- vitest-cucumber makes each step a separate test
- Stryker reruns only the tests that ran the mutated line: the **When**, without its **Then**
- so `vitest.config.mutation.ts` leaves the BDD steps out, as upstream's does

The scenario proves the requirement in `pnpm test`. A **unit test** pins the code under mutation. Talk 2.

---

## Lean: specify first removes rework

| Without specify first | The waste |
| --- | --- |
| code written, then argued about | **rework** |
| a test written after, from the code's own output | a **hidden defect**: the test agrees with the bug |
| behaviour nobody asked for | **extra features** |
| "what was this supposed to do?" six months on | **relearning** |

The sentence is approved before the code; the scenario is red before the code; the closure keeps them
together after.

---

## Take away

- feature, Rule, scenario, step: each has one job
- a Rule repeats its requirement word for word, tagged with its id and pattern
- write the scenario before the code, and see it fail for the right reason
- the scenario proves the rule; a unit or property test pins the code
- Specify first is the standard work for weeks 7 to 11
